window.docContents[156].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___13___-Hilbert___s-third-problem/Section-2___13___-Hilbert___s-third-problem-___-heading/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___13_heading":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.13. Hilbert's third problem\u0009Section 2.13: Hilbert's third problem — heading","header":"Section 2.13: Hilbert's third problem — heading","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___13___-Hilbert___s-third-problem/Section-2___13___-Hilbert___s-third-problem-___-heading/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___13_heading"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/Dual-representation-of-a-Lie-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras--Dual-representation-of-a-Lie-algebra--Dual-representation-of-a-Lie-algebra--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Evaluating the bracket of a Lie element with an auxiliary element gives the negated evaluation at the bracket of that Lie element with the vector.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.14.2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.14. Tensor products and duals of representations of Lie algebras\u0009Dual representation of a Lie algebra\u0009Dual representation of a Lie algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/Dual-representation-of-a-Lie-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras--Dual-representation-of-a-Lie-algebra--Dual-representation-of-a-Lie-algebra--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Unit-in-an-associative-algebra/#Chapter2___Definition2___2___2":{"contents":"Definition 2.2.2. A unit in an associative algebra A is an element 1 \\in A such that 1a = a1 = a.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.2. Algebras\u0009Unit in an associative algebra","header":"Unit in an associative algebra","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Unit-in-an-associative-algebra/#Chapter2___Definition2___2___2"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___5___-Quotients/Cyclic-representations/#Chapter2___Problem2___5___2":{"contents":"Problem 2.5.2. Let V \\neq 0 be a representation of A. We say that a vector v \\in V is cyclic if it generates V, i.e., Av = V. A representation admitting a cyclic vector is said to be cyclic. Show the following:\n\n(a) V is irreducible if and only if all nonzero vectors of V are cyclic.\n\n(b) V is cyclic if and only if it is isomorphic to A/I, where I is a left ideal in A.\n\n(c) Give an example of an indecomposable representation which is not cyclic.\n\nHint: Let A = \\mathbb{C}[x, y]/I_2, where I_2 is the ideal spanned by homogeneous polynomials of degree \\geq 2 (so A has a basis 1, x, y). Let V = A^* be the space of linear functionals on A, with the action of A given by (\\rho(a)f)(b) = f(ba). Show that V provides such an example.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.5. Quotients\u0009Cyclic representations","header":"Cyclic representations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___5___-Quotients/Cyclic-representations/#Chapter2___Problem2___5___2"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___2___-Characters/Fewer-irreducible-representations-when-___G___-___-0-in-k/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___2___-Characters--Fewer-irreducible-representations-when-___G___-___-0-in-k--Fewer-irreducible-representations-when-___G___-___-0-in-k--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"For an algebraically closed field in which the finite group's order maps to zero, the auxiliary type has smaller cardinality than its conjugacy-class type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Exercise4.2.3; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.2. Characters\u0009Fewer irreducible representations when |G| = 0 in k\u0009Fewer irreducible representations when |G| = 0 in k\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___2___-Characters/Fewer-irreducible-representations-when-___G___-___-0-in-k/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___2___-Characters--Fewer-irreducible-representations-when-___G___-___-0-in-k--Fewer-irreducible-representations-when-___G___-___-0-in-k--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___2___-Characters/Number-of-irreducible-representations-equals-number-of-conjugacy-classes/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___2___-Characters--Number-of-irreducible-representations-equals-number-of-conjugacy-classes--Number-of-irreducible-representations-equals-number-of-conjugacy-classes--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"There is a complete finite family of pairwise nonisomorphic simple finite-dimensional representations whose cardinality equals the number of conjugacy classes.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Corollary4.2.2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.2. Characters\u0009Number of irreducible representations equals number of conjugacy classes\u0009Number of irreducible representations equals number of conjugacy classes\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___2___-Characters/Number-of-irreducible-representations-equals-number-of-conjugacy-classes/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___2___-Characters--Number-of-irreducible-representations-equals-number-of-conjugacy-classes--Number-of-irreducible-representations-equals-number-of-conjugacy-classes--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___6___-Unitary-representations___-Another-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/Existence-and-uniqueness-of-unitary-structure-for-finite-groups/#Chapter4___Theorem4___6___2":{"contents":"Theorem 4.6.2. If G is finite, then any finite dimensional representation of G has a unitary structure. If the representation is irreducible, this structure is unique up to scaling by a positive real number.\n\nProof. Take any positive definite Hermitian form B on V and define another Hermitian form \\mathbf{B} on V as follows:\n\n\\mathbf{B}(v, w) = \\sum_{g \\in G} B(\\rho_V(g)v, \\rho_V(g)w).\n\nThen \\mathbf{B} is a positive definite G-invariant Hermitian form on V.\n\nIf V is an irreducible representation and B_1, B_2 are two positive definite G-invariant Hermitian forms on V, then B_1(v, w) = B_2(Av, w) for some linear map A : V \\to V (since any positive definite Hermitian form is nondegenerate), and moreover A is also G-invariant, i.e., is a homomorphism of representations. Then by Schur's lemma, A = \\lambda \\mathrm{Id}, and clearly \\lambda > 0. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.6. Unitary representations. Another proof of Maschke's theorem for complex representations\u0009Existence and uniqueness of unitary structure for finite groups","header":"Existence and uniqueness of unitary structure for finite groups","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___6___-Unitary-representations___-Another-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/Existence-and-uniqueness-of-unitary-structure-for-finite-groups/#Chapter4___Theorem4___6___2"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Theorem-6___8___1___-Dimension-vector-eventually-becomes-alpha_p/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem--Theorem-6___8___1___-Dimension-vector-eventually-becomes-alpha_p--Theorem-6___8___1___-Dimension-vector-eventually-becomes-alpha_p--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Under the displayed hypotheses, there exist a vertex list, a finite index, a second quiver, and an auxiliary representation satisfying the stated equalities and finiteness conditions.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Theorem6.8.1; role=supporting]\n\nA nonnegative nonzero integer vector of quadratic value two can be carried to a coordinate vector by a finite sequence of coordinate reflections.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Theorem6.8.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.8. Proof of Gabriel's theorem\u0009Theorem 6.8.1: Dimension vector eventually becomes alpha_p\u0009Theorem 6.8.1: Dimension vector eventually becomes alpha_p\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Theorem-6___8___1___-Dimension-vector-eventually-becomes-alpha_p/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem--Theorem-6___8___1___-Dimension-vector-eventually-becomes-alpha_p--Theorem-6___8___1___-Dimension-vector-eventually-becomes-alpha_p--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/Backmatter/References-for-historical-interludes/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--Backmatter--References-for-historical-interludes":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u0009Backmatter","header":"References for historical interludes","id":"/Backmatter/References-for-historical-interludes/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--Backmatter--References-for-historical-interludes"}});